1 NOTE <b>Sylvarachne</b>
2 CONT <p>("Sylva" for forest, reflecting the arboreal nature of Phasmatodea, and "Arachne" for spinning, symbolizing the intricate web-spinning of Embioptera.)<b>Myth of Sylvarachne:</b>
2 CONT <p>Long ago, in the time of the <b>Verdant Weave</b>, when the forests of the world stretched unbroken and teemed with life, there lived <b>Sylvarachne</b>, the Forest Weaver. Born of <b>Arborea</b>, the spirit of the living woods, and <b>Fibrel
2 CONC lus</b>, the god of threads and binding, Sylvarachne was a being of quiet artistry and endless patience.<b>The Legend of the Eternal Tapestry:</b>
2 CONT <p>Sylvarachne was tasked by their parents to create the <b>Eternal Tapestry</b>, a work of living art that would preserve the balance of the forest. It was said that the tapestry would be woven from the strands of moonlight, dew, and shadow, an
2 CONC d would hang between the trees to unite all creatures of the woods.
2 CONT <p>1.</p>
2 CONT <p><b>The Dance of Shadows (Phasmatodea):</b></p>
2 CONT <p>To gather the strands of shadow, Sylvarachne disguised themselves as a branch, blending into the forest unnoticed. This skill of perfect mimicry allowed them to gather the soft, elusive shadows cast by moonlight. From this act, the <b>Stick I
2 CONC nsects</b> emerged, masters of camouflage who live in harmony with the trees.
2 CONT <p>2.</p>
2 CONT <p><b>The Loom of Life (Embioptera):</b></p>
2 CONT <p>To weave the strands into a tapestry, Sylvarachne spun threads of unparalleled strength and delicacy, using their silk to bind the elements together. This weaving birthed the <b>Webspinners</b>, creatures who became architects of intricate we
2 CONC bs, crafting shelters that protect their kin.<b>The Legacy of Sylvarachne:</b>
2 CONT <p>Once the Eternal Tapestry was completed, it was said that Sylvarachne wove themselves into it, becoming one with the forest and imbuing their descendants with their gifts:
2 CONT <p>• The <b>Phasmatodea</b> carry the essence of shadows and mimicry, blending seamlessly into their surroundings.
2 CONT <p>• The <b>Embioptera</b> inherited the craft of weaving and the ingenuity to create homes of silk.
2 CONT <p>The Eternal Tapestry is believed to still exist, hidden deep in the oldest forests. Legends say that those who find it will glimpse the interconnectedness of all life, a reminder of Sylvarachne’s legacy as the ancestor who bound the natural w
2 CONC orld together.
