1 NOTE {geni:about_me} tory of Urvashi and Pururava Apsaras are the celestial maidens of Indian mythology. They are not to be confused with angels. They are extremely beautiful immortals who live in heaven and entertain the demi-Gods with their dancin
2 CONC g and singing. Urvashi is one such apsara. There are many legends about her birth but the following one is most prevalent.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Once the revered sage Narnarayan was meditating in the holy shrine of Badrinath situated in the Himalayas. Indra, the king of the demi-Gods, did not want the sage to acquire divine powers through the meditation and sent two apsaras to distrac
2 CONC t him.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>The sage struck his thigh and created a woman so beautiful that Indra's apsaras were left matchless. This was Urvashi, named from ur, the Sanskrit word for thigh. After his meditation was complete the sage gifted Urvashi to Indra, and she occ
2 CONC upied the pride of place in Indra's court.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Atri was one of Brahma's seven sons who married Bhadra. He later went on to conceive a son named Som or the Moon. Som was a very attractive young man and eloped with Sage Brahaspati's wife, Tara. Som and Tara gave birth to Buddh during the ab
2 CONC sence of Brahaspati.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Vaivasvata Manu's daughter Ila fell in love with Buddh. They together conceived a son named Pururuva. Pururuva became a great king and established the Lunar Dynasty named after his grandfather. Ages later, in his incarnation as Krishna, Vishn
2 CONC u took birth in this dynasty.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Urvashi found the atmosphere in heaven stifling. Everything was cold and synthetic including the colours and the fragrances of the flowers. Urvashi often stole to the Earth at night with her friends to feel the wet dew under her feet and th
2 CONC e soft breeze against her body. On the other hand Pururava envied the Gods.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>He was a regular invitee to Indra's court and was haunted at night by the grandeur he saw there. He would then take his chariot above the clouds and hurtle through the skies at break-neck speed. It was on such an occasion that the two met.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Urvashi was returning to heaven just before dawn with the other apsaras, when she was abducted by a demon. Pururava saw this and chased the demon on his chariot and freed Urvashi from his clutches. The brief period their bodies touched change
2 CONC d their lives forever.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>For the first time Urvasi experienced the warm flesh of a mortal, for the first time she heard blood pounding in veins and for the first time she heard the inhalation and exhalation of breath. </p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Pururava had seen Urvashi in Indra's court before and to actually be close to the most beautiful woman in heaven gave him a high. Pururava left Urvashi with her friends but when they parted each was madly in love with the other but unsure whe
2 CONC ther the love was being reciprocated.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Urvashi was a woman in a man's world and in keeping with the tradition of the times expected the man to make the first move. Pururava on the other hand feared rejection because he did not expect the pride of heaven to come and live with a mor
2 CONC tal, and hence did not approach Urvashi. So both pined for each other. Urvashi was giving a dance performance in which she was portraying Vishnu's consort, Laxmi.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Her concentration was with Pururava and she called out her lover's name instead of saying "Vishnu". Her teacher, the sage Bharat, got offended and cursed Urvashi. "You will get to live with the person you are thinking about," he said, "And yo
2 CONC u will also give birth to his son. But you will have to choose between the father and son, because the day they see each other you will have to leave them both and return to heaven."
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>The curse actually emboldened Urvashi. She wasn't even thinking about children, she was sure to get her love. She sent a friend to earth to find out about Pururava. The friend located the king in the garden of Gandhmadan (meaning intoxicatin
2 CONC g fragrance), whining away for his ladylove. The sage was set. Urvashi left heaven and went to the waiting arms of Pururava.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>--------------------</p>
2 CONT <p>Urvashi From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia "Urvasi" redirects here. For the song, see Kadhalan (soundtrack). This article is about character in a Hindu legend. For other uses, see Urvashi (disambiguation). Urvashi Urvashi Urvashi and Purur
2 CONC avas painted by Raja Ravi Varma Information Children	Rishyasringa from Vibhandaka Ayu from king Pururavas Urvashi (Sanskrit: उर्वशी, lit. she who can control heart of others. ("Ur" means heart and "vash" means to control) is an Apsara (nymph) i
2 CONC n Hindu legend. Monier Monier-Williams proposes a different etymology in which the name means 'widely pervasive' and suggests that in its first appearances in Vedic texts it is a name for the dawn goddess. She was a celestial maiden in Indra's c
2 CONC ourt and was considered the most beautiful of all the Apsaras.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>She is mother of Rishyasringa great saint of the Ramayan era of ancient India from Vibhandaka who later played crucial role in birth of Rama and was married to Shanta elder sister of Ram.</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>She became the wife of king Pururavas (Purūrávas, from purū+rávas "crying much or loudly"), an ancient chief of the lunar race. ShBr 11.5.1, and treated in Kalidasa's drama Vikramōrvaśīyam.</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>She is perennially youthful and infinitely charming but always elusive.[1] She is a source as much of delight as of dolour.[2]</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Birth[edit]</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Narayana on the left and Nara on the right, Deogarh, Uttar Pradesh, ca. 5th century There are many legends about the birth of Urvashi but the following one is most prevalent.</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Once the revered sages Nara-narayana was meditating in the holy shrine of Badrinath Temple situated in the Himalayas. Indra, the king of the Gods, did not want the sage to acquire divine powers through meditation and sent two apsaras to distr
2 CONC act him. The sage struck his thigh and created a woman so beautiful that Indra’s apsaras were left matchless. This was Urvashi, named from ur, the Sanskrit word for thigh. After his meditation was complete the sage gifted Urvashi to Indra, and s
2 CONC he occupied the pride of place in Indra’s court.
