1 NOTE {geni:about_me} HE WAS A FAMOUS KING WHO CONQUERED MOST OF INDIA. THE GREAT EPIC RAGHUVAMSA DESCRIBE HIS VICTORIES.AFTER HIM THE SUN DYNASTY WAS ALSO KNOWN AS DYNASTY OD RAGHU AS RAGHAV (RAGHUVANSI)
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Raghuvaṃśa This is article is about the epic poem by Kalidasa. For other uses, see Raghuvamsa (disambiguation). Raghuvamsa (Sanskrit: रघुवंश, Raghuvaṃśa) is a Sanskrit mahakavya (epic poem) by the most celebrated Sanskrit poet Kalidasa. It na
2 CONC rrates, in 19 sargas (cantos), the stories related to the Raghu dynasty, namely the family of Dilipa and his descendants up to Agnivarna, who include Raghu,Dasharatha and Rama. The earliest surviving commentary written on the work is that of th
2 CONC e 10th-century Kashmirian scholar Vallabhadeva.[1] The most popular and widely available commentary, however, is the Sanjivani, written by Mallinatha (ca.1350-1450). • Geographical and historical references warrior Raghu leads a military expedit
2 CONC ion to Transoxiana. He defeats and subjugates local people along the way (presumably on his march through Central Asia) until he reaches the Vankshu, as the ancient Indians called the Oxus River. There, Raghu's army battles the Hepthalites, or W
2 CONC hite Huns, whom the Indians called Hunas and Mlecchas (barbarians). The Hepthalites are defeated, and theRaghuvamsha boasts of "The exploits of Raghu, whose valor expressed itself amongst the husbands of the Huna women, became manifest in the sc
2 CONC arlet color of their cheeks." After crossing the Oxus, Raghu and his army encountered the Kambojas, an ancient Indo-Scythian people often mentioned in Indian texts. The Kambojas submitted to Raghu and offered him gifts and treasures. Evidently
2 CONC , the Kambojas dwelt in the vicinity of the Pamirs. Kalidasa describes the preponderance of walnut trees in the Oxus country, this particular region is still known for the cultivation of walnuts. Metres used in the epic  The epic is composed i
2 CONC n 21 Sanskrit metres, namely Anuṣṭup, Indravajrā, Upajāti, Upendravajrā, Aupacchandasika, Toṭaka, Drutavilambita, Puṣpitāgrā, Praharṣiṇī, Mañjubhāṣiṇī, Mattamayūra, Mandākrāntā, Mālinī, Rathoddhatā, Vaṃśastha, Vasantatilakā, Vaitāl��������������
2 CONC ����ya, Śārdūlavikrīḍita, Śālinī, Svāgatā, Hariṇī.[2]
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p> • </p>
2 CONT <p> • </p>
2 CONT <p></p>
