2 CONT <p></p>
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was the eldest son of Mursili II and Queen Gassulawiya, and he had several siblings.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>He is best known as the Hittite ruler who fought Ramesses II to a standstill at the Battle of Kadesh around 1274 BC. Aside from the battle with Egypt, he is best known for relocating the Hittite capital to Tarhuntassa[1] and appointing his br
2 CONC other Hattusili as governor in Hattusa.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>A copy of a treaty has been recovered between him and Alaksandu, ruler of Wilusa (Troy), one of the Arzawa lands.</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muwatalli_II</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Muwatalli II</p>
2 CONT <p>From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Muwatalli II was a king of the New kingdom of the Hittite empire (1295–1272 BC). The eldest surviving son of Mursili II, he is best known as the Hittite ruler who fought Ramesses II to a standstill at the Battle of Kadesh around 1274 BC. Afte
2 CONC r becoming king, he relocated the capitol to Tarhuntassa when the Kaskas invaded; appointing his brother Hattusili as governor in Hattusa.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>A copy of a treaty between him and Alaksandu, ruler of Wilusa, one of the Arzawa lands has been recovered. Egyptologists also believe that Muwattalli reached an informal peace treaty or understanding with Seti I over Kadesh to avoid a clash b
2 CONC etween the two superpowers over control of Syria. In it, Seti I effectively ceded Kadesh to the Hittite king in order to focus on domestic issues in Egypt.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Muwatalli had at least two children, both given Hurrian names in honor of the Hurrian storm god Teshup. One was a son, Urhi-Teshup, who became king Mursili III until Hattusili III deposed him. Another was possibly a daughter, Ulmi-Teshup ("fe
2 CONC male-slave of Teshup"); whom Hattusili III either married to king Kurunta in Tarhuntassa, or else (if male) was renamed Kurunta and appointed.
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Muwatalli's namesake, Muwatalli I, was a pre-Empire king of the early 14th century, the predecessor of Tudhaliya I.</p>
2 CONT <p>[edit]</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>External links</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>    * Reign of Muwatalli II</p>
2 CONT <p></p>
2 CONT <p>Preceded by:</p>
2 CONT <p>Mursili II Hittite king Succeeded by:</p>
2 CONT <p>Mursili III</p>
